(vector (map (:adr/consequences "{:reusable_foundation \"18 genre-labelled starting points now exist for any\\n                         future kotoba-lang game project to fork from,\\n                         each with documented, honestly-scoped engine\\n                         constraints rather than aspirational unverified\\n                         claims\", :discipline_precedent \"the honest N/A-vs-real-mechanic distinction\\n                          methodology (4/18 categories) is now a concrete,\\n                          citable precedent for any future taxonomy-driven\\n                          content generation task in this org -- 'hit every\\n                          requested slot' does not override 'do not\\n                          fabricate'\", :follow_up_debt \"WASM-compile verification for all 18 games remains\\n                    undone; a natural next pass once genuinely needed by a\\n                    real consuming game project, not speculative work now\"}"), (:adr/authoritative_for (vector "kotoba-lang/kami-genre-base-systems holds base/foundation gameplay\n    systems, one per genre category from a PlayStation-Store-style\n    genre-browse taxonomy (generic genre LABELS only, never any specific\n    commercial title's characters/art/story/mechanics)" "18 categories were requested; not all are gameplay-mechanic archetypes\n    -- 4 (modern-remake/free-to-play/indie/post-apocalyptic) are\n    business/production/setting categories with no distinct core loop of\n    their own, and this repo does not force-invent a fake mechanic to claim\n    18/18 identical-shape 'real systems' -- each of those 4 instead gets a\n    real, honestly-labelled SUPPORTING SYSTEM a game in that category would\n    plausibly need (a tuning-preset layer, a currency/economy loop, a\n    minimal starter kernel, a resource-scarcity system), not a claim that\n    the category itself is a mechanic" "all 18 categories now have real, clojure -M author.clj-verified\n    author.clj+logic.clj pairs, built strictly within the kami-clj compiler\n    subset's proven vocabulary (cross-checked against every reference game\n    this session established: kami-clj-play/games/{survivors,waves},\n    kami-clj-play3d/games/{royale,rt-audio-demo,dance})" "local-multiplayer and fighting both use the same honest '1 human + 1\n    AI' adaptation isekai-network's own concept-3 investigation established\n    as the only currently-buildable local-multiplayer shape, given the\n    confirmed single-local-input-stream engine constraint" "a maturity pass added 1-3 additional defsystems/richer state per\n    category (scoped honestly, not padded for parity) deepening each\n    genre's core loop beyond its bare minimal proving mechanic")), (:adr/supersedes (vector)), (:adr/superseded_by (vector)), (:adr/topic "kami-genre-base-systems"), (:adr/related (vector "CLAUDE.md" "90-docs/adr/2607023200-isekai-network-standalone-repo.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607031700-kotoba-lang-engine-wasm-emission-and-host-runtime.edn" "gftdcojp/isekai-network (the precedent this repo's author.clj/logic.clj\n    shape and honesty discipline follows)" "kotoba-lang/kami-genre-base-systems")), (:adr/status "accepted"), (:adr/id "adr-2607031800-kami-genre-base-systems"), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:adr/last_verified "2026-07-03"), (:adr/body "# ADR 2607031800: kami-genre-base-systems — 18 genre-category base gameplay systems for kotoba-lang\n\n## Status\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThe user pasted a PlayStation Store genre-browse page (real commercial\ntitles as genre examples), asked for just the category labels extracted,\nthen asked for a base gameplay system per category \"as kotoba-lang.\" The\nextraction step cleanly separated two things the original paste mixed:\nreal commercial game titles/key-art descriptions (copyrighted, never\nreproduced or mimicked) and generic genre category labels (not\ncopyrightable, safe as organizing labels for wholly original systems).\n\nThe kami-clj compiler subset's proven vocabulary, established cumulatively\nacross this session's isekai-network work, is genuinely constrained: a\nsingle local input stream (no player-index axis argument anywhere), no\nterrain/tilemap/pathfinding/vehicle-physics/skeletal-animation reachable\nfrom guest logic (Rust-substrate only), and — checked before use, not\nassumed — no multiplication operator confirmed anywhere in any reference\ngame or prior verified base system. Every system in this repo was built to\nfit inside these real constraints. Separately, 4 of the 18 requested\ncategories (modern-remake, free-to-play, indie, post-apocalyptic) are not\ngameplay archetypes at all — forcing them into the same \"distinct core\nloop\" shape as the other 14 would have meant fabricating fictional\nmechanics with no grounding.\n\n## Decision\n\n### Repo structure\n\n`kotoba-lang/kami-genre-base-systems` (public): `design/<genre>.edn` (one\ndesign doc per category) + `games/<genre>/{author.clj,logic.clj,deps.edn,\n.gitignore}` (one pair per category with real code) — mirrors\n`kami-clj-play/games/`'s established convention exactly.\n\n### 14 genuine gameplay archetypes, each with real code and a distinct core loop\n\nrhythm (beat-gated `tick-n`+`mod` timing, isekai-network's own proven\npattern; maturity pass added a combo/streak counter) · puzzle (maturity\npass added a second distinct tile-arrangement variant for real progression)\n· stealth (maturity pass replaced binary detected/not with an escalating\nalert-level that decays over time) · single-player (canonical\nsurvivors-shaped reference loop + a level counter) · strategy (maturity\npass added a real win/lose condition via opposing force counters) · sports\n(ball-and-goal proximity) · horror (escalating pursuit tension) ·\nsuperhero (empowered combat reskin) · open-world (large flat arena +\nscattered discovery, honestly not streaming terrain — that's Rust-substrate\nonly) · local-multiplayer (1 human + 1 AI vs a shared objective, the honest\nfallback for the confirmed single-input-stream constraint) · kids-family\n(deliberately the simplest base — zero-fail stationary collection, no chase\nAI) · relaxing (player-paced touch-to-grow loop, no timer pressure) ·\nplatformer (sequential ordered-waypoint traversal — honestly not a\njump-physics platformer; no jump/gravity primitive exists anywhere in the\nproven vocabulary, confirmed via a targeted grep before concluding this,\nnot assumed) · fighting (1 human + 1 AI opponent, same input-stream\nconstraint as local-multiplayer, proximity-exchange health counters).\n\n### 4 honest supporting systems, not fabricated mechanics\n\n- **modern-remake**: a real, reusable tuning-preset system (wider\n  hit-tolerance, auto-aim-style larger weapon-range) demonstrated by\n  re-tuning `single-player`'s constants — not a fabricated \"remake\n  mechanic.\"\n- **free-to-play**: a real soft-currency economy loop (earn via enemy\n  defeat, auto-spend at a threshold for a temporary buff that decays) —\n  a genuine common F2P building block, not a claim that F2P is itself a\n  mechanic.\n- **indie**: the smallest possible starter kernel in the repo (one entity,\n  one movement system, nothing else) — a genuinely useful rapid-iteration\n  starting point, explicitly labelled a scaffold, not a genre mechanic.\n- **post-apocalyptic**: a real resource-scarcity system (limited weapon\n  \"charges\" depleting per use, replenished by scarce salvage pickups) — a\n  genuine scarcity mechanic, not a claim that the narrative setting is\n  inherently a distinct core loop.\n\n### Verification\n\nEvery `author.clj` actually run via `clojure -M author.clj`, confirmed\nproducing valid `scene.edn`; every `logic.clj` construct cross-checked\nagainst the proven vocabulary. Multiplication (`*`) was checked and found\nnot confirmed anywhere in any reference game or prior verified base, so\nperiodic-timing constants use precomputed values with a derivation comment\nrather than an unverified `*` call. WASM compilation (`kotoba-lang/engine`'s\n`compile-file`) was **not** attempted for all 18 in this pass — a\nlegitimate, valuable follow-up, out of scope here given this session's\nestablished slow-build cost for that verification tier.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- 18 genre-labelled starting points now exist for any future kotoba-lang\n  game project to fork from, each with documented, honestly-scoped engine\n  constraints rather than aspirational unverified claims.\n- The honest N/A-vs-real-mechanic distinction methodology (4/18 categories)\n  is now a concrete, citable precedent: \"hit every requested slot\" does not\n  override \"do not fabricate.\"\n- WASM-compile verification for all 18 games remains a real follow-up debt,\n  not silently claimed as done.\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n1. **Force all 18 into an identical \"distinct core loop\" shape**, inventing\n   mechanics for the 4 business/setting categories: rejected — would have\n   fabricated ungrounded gameplay claims, the same failure mode this\n   session's isekai-network design-investigation work repeatedly avoided.\n2. **Skip the 4 non-mechanic categories entirely**: rejected — the user\n   explicitly asked for real code for these too; the honest resolution was\n   a real, clearly-labelled supporting system per category, not silence.\n3. **Attempt real pathfinding/physics/vehicle integration** for genres that\n   traditionally need it: rejected — confirmed Rust-substrate-only,\n   unreachable from kami-clj guest logic, via this session's repeated\n   concept-level investigations for isekai-network.\n\n## References\n\n- ADR-2607023200 (isekai-network standalone repo — the honesty-discipline\n  precedent this repo follows)\n- ADR-2607031700 (kotoba-lang/engine + kami-script-runtime-rs — the\n  compile/run pipeline a future WASM-verification pass for this repo would\n  use)\n- `kotoba-lang/kami-genre-base-systems`"), (:adr/decision "{:repo {:path \"kotoba-lang/kami-genre-base-systems\", :visibility :public, :structure \"design/<genre>.edn (one design doc per category,\\n                     schema: :genre/id :genre/core-loop :genre/controls\\n                     :genre/kami-engine{:proven-vocab-used :known-constraints})\\n                     + games/<genre>/{author.clj,logic.clj,deps.edn,.gitignore}\\n                     (one pair per category with real code) -- mirrors\\n                     kami-clj-play/games/'s established convention exactly\"}, :fourteen_gameplay_archetypes [\"rhythm (beat-gated tick-n+mod timing, the pattern isekai-network's own\\n     concept 1/4 proved and this repo's rhythm system directly reuses) --\\n     maturity pass added a defatom combo/streak counter\" \"puzzle -- maturity pass added a second distinct tile-arrangement\\n     variant so there is real progression, not one static puzzle\" \"stealth -- maturity pass replaced binary detected/not-detected with an\\n     escalating defatom alert-level that rises on detection and decays over\\n     time\" \"single-player (the canonical survivors-shaped reference loop plus a\\n     defatom level counter escalating spawn tightness)\" \"strategy -- maturity pass added a real win/lose condition (opposing\\n     defatom force counters, game ends at zero)\" \"sports (ball-and-goal proximity mechanic)\" \"horror (escalating pursuit-speed tension loop)\" \"superhero (empowered/wide-range combat, themed reskin of the base\\n     weapon system)\" \"open-world (large flat arena + scattered point-of-interest discovery,\\n     honestly NOT streaming terrain -- that fidelity is Rust-substrate\\n     only)\" \"local-multiplayer (1 human + 1 AI vs a shared ball/objective, honest\\n     fallback for the confirmed single-input-stream constraint)\" \"kids-family (deliberately the simplest base in the repo -- zero-fail\\n     stationary-collection loop, no chase AI at all)\" \"relaxing (player-paced 'garden' touch-to-grow loop, no timer pressure)\" \"platformer (sequential ordered-waypoint traversal -- honestly NOT a\\n     jump-physics platformer; no jump/gravity primitive exists anywhere in\\n     the proven vocabulary, confirmed via a targeted grep across every\\n     reference game before concluding this)\" \"fighting (1 human + 1 AI opponent, same input-stream constraint as\\n     local-multiplayer, proximity-exchange defatom health counters)\"], :four_supporting_systems_not_mechanics [\"modern-remake -- a real, reusable TUNING-PRESET system (wider hit-\\n     tolerance, auto-aim-style larger weapon-range) demonstrated by\\n     re-tuning single-player's constants, not a fabricated 'remake\\n     mechanic'\" \"free-to-play -- a real soft-currency economy loop (earn via enemy\\n     defeat, auto-spend at a threshold for a temporary buff, buff decays)\\n     layered on single-player -- a genuine common F2P building block, not a\\n     claim that F2P is itself a mechanic\" \"indie -- the smallest possible starter kernel in the repo (one entity,\\n     one movement system, nothing else) -- a genuinely useful rapid-\\n     iteration starting point, explicitly labelled as a scaffold not a\\n     genre mechanic\" \"post-apocalyptic -- a real resource-scarcity system (limited weapon\\n     defatom 'charges' depleting per use, replenished by scarce 'salvage'\\n     pickups) layered on single-player -- a genuine scarcity mechanic, not\\n     a claim that the narrative setting is inherently a distinct core loop\"], :verification \"every author.clj actually run via `clojure -M author.clj`, confirmed\\n   producing valid scene.edn; every logic.clj construct cross-checked\\n   against the proven vocabulary (defsystem/defn/def/let/when/cond/if/f32/\\n   spawn-entity/despawn-entity/set-position!/set-velocity!/get-x/get-y/\\n   nearest-tagged/count-tagged/doseq-entities/move-toward!/axis/tick-n/mod/\\n   rand-int/zero?/</=/not=/defatom/set-atom!) -- multiplication was checked\\n   and found NOT confirmed anywhere in any reference game or prior verified\\n   base, so score-reset-style periods use precomputed constants with a\\n   comment explaining the derivation, rather than an unverified `*` call.\\n   WASM compilation (kotoba-lang/engine's compile-file) was NOT attempted\\n   for all 18 in this pass -- a legitimate, valuable follow-up, out of\\n   scope here given this session's established slow-build cost for that\\n   verification tier\"}"), (:db/id -1), (:adr/problem "{:source \"user request: pasted a PlayStation Store genre-browse page\\n            (real commercial titles as genre examples), asked for just the\\n            category labels extracted, then asked for a base gameplay\\n            system per category 'as kotoba-lang'\", :investigated [\"the pasted list mixed two things: real commercial game titles/key-art\\n    descriptions (copyrighted, must not be reproduced/mimicked) and generic\\n    genre category labels (not copyrightable, safe to use as organizing\\n    labels for wholly original systems) -- the category-label-only\\n    extraction the user explicitly requested resolved this cleanly before\\n    any system-building began\" \"the kami-clj compiler subset's proven vocabulary, established\\n    cumulatively across this whole session's isekai-network work, is\\n    genuinely constrained: single local input stream (no player-index axis\\n    argument anywhere), no terrain/tilemap/pathfinding/vehicle-physics/\\n    skeletal-animation reachable from guest logic (Rust-substrate only),\\n    no multiplication operator confirmed anywhere in any reference game or\\n    prior verified base system (checked before use, not assumed) -- every\\n    system in this repo was built to fit inside these real constraints,\\n    not an idealized unconstrained design\" \"4 of the 18 requested categories are not gameplay archetypes at all --\\n    forcing them into the same 'distinct core loop' shape as the other 14\\n    would have meant fabricating fictional mechanics with no grounding,\\n    the same failure mode this session's design-investigation work (concept\\n    2-5 open-questions) repeatedly rejected for isekai-network itself\"], :risk \"18 base systems is a large surface -- risk of quality dilution\\n         (shallow, near-identical copies) or risk of dishonest padding on\\n         the 4 non-mechanic categories to hit a round number. Both were\\n         explicitly guarded against: real per-genre design distinctions\\n         (see :decision below), and honest N/A/preset framing for the 4\\n         business/setting categories rather than fabricated mechanics\"}"), (:adr/alternatives "[{:option \"force all 18 categories into an identical 'distinct core loop'\\n            shape, inventing mechanics for the 4 business/setting\\n            categories\", :rejected_because \"would have fabricated ungrounded gameplay claims for\\n                       categories that are not gameplay archetypes -- the\\n                       same failure mode this session's isekai-network\\n                       design-investigation work (concept 2-5) repeatedly\\n                       and deliberately avoided\"} {:option \"skip the 4 non-mechanic categories entirely (no games/ dir,\\n            no code at all)\", :rejected_because \"the user explicitly asked for real code for these 4\\n                       too -- the honest resolution was a real, clearly-\\n                       labelled supporting system per category, not silence\"} {:option \"attempt real hecs/pathfinding/physics integration for genres\\n            that traditionally need it (strategy pathfinding, platformer\\n            physics, sports vehicles) instead of the gameplay-logic-only\\n            approximation\", :rejected_because \"these are Rust-substrate crates unreachable from\\n                       kami-clj guest logic -- confirmed, not assumed, via\\n                       this session's repeated concept-level investigations\\n                       for isekai-network. Attempting it here would have\\n                       required new host-side engine work out of scope for\\n                       a base-systems content repo\"}]"), (:adr/authoritative true), (:adr/title "ADR-2607031800: kami-genre-base-systems -- 18 genre-category base gameplay systems for kotoba-lang"), (:adr/source-format "edn")))